Elvis 1.4, by Steve Kirkendall.

The grant is Kirkendall's own and has always been in this directory; it was
not added by this port.  It is in README, lines 9-10, reproduced here verbatim:

    Elvis is freely redistributable, in either source form or executable form.
    There are no restrictions on how you may use it.

The program states the same thing about itself at run time.  vi.h line 14:

    #define COPYING	"This version of ELVIS is freely redistributable."

and doc/intro.doc repeats it in the manual.

The version is Elvis 1.4 (vi.h line 13, `#define VERSION "ELVIS 1.4, by Steve
Kirkendall"'; corroborated by doc/versions.doc).  This matters, because
Kirkendall's terms changed over the life of elvis: his later releases are
narrower ("freely redistributable, subject to the restrictions noted in the
documentation"), and later still the Artistic/GPL line.  The unrestricted
wording above is 1.4's, and 1.4 is what this is.


ONE FILE IS NOT COVERED BY THE ABOVE
------------------------------------

osk.c carries a different and narrower notice, at lines 8-15:

    |     Copyright (c) 1988 by Wolfgang Ocker, Puchheim,
    |                           Ulli Dessauer, Germering and
    |                           Reimer Mellin, Muenchen
    |                           (W-Germany)
    |
    |  This  programm can  be  copied and  distributed freely  for any
    |  non-commercial  purposes.   It can only  be  incorporated  into
    |  commercial software with the written permission of the authors.

osk.c is the OS-9/68000 port layer.  Nothing in this project uses it -- the
Makefile's only reference to it is commented out (`#EXTRA2=	osk$O') -- and it
is the sole non-commercial restriction in 169 elvis files.  It is retained
here only because this audit removes nothing; deleting it would end the
restriction outright.
